<p><a href=3D"https://developers.arcgis.com/qt/cpp/api-reference/esri-arcgi=
sruntime-analysis.html"><code>Analysis</code></a> and <a href=3D"https://de=
velopers.arcgis.com/qt/cpp/api-reference/esri-arcgisruntime-analysisoverlay=
.html"><code>AnalysisOverlay</code></a> classes provide fast and dynamic an=
alysis results in a scene view. The analysis classes allow you to define an=
alyses (such as viewshed, line of sight, and measure) that use data in the =
current scene and render results dynamically in the view. Analysis results =
are displayed using an <a href=3D"https://developers.arcgis.com/qt/cpp/api-=
reference/esri-arcgisruntime-analysisoverlay.html"><code>AnalysisOverlay</c=
ode></a> in a scene view. For more information about working with analysis =
overlays, see <a href=3D"https://developers.arcgis.com/qt/spatial-and-data-=
analysis/manage-analysis-with-analysis-overlays/">Manage analysis with anal=
ysis overlays</a>.</p><p>You can use the <a href=3D"https://developers.arcg=
is.com/qt/cpp/api-reference/esri-arcgisruntime-locationdistancemeasurement.=
html"><code>LocationDistanceMeasurement</code></a> analysis class to make i=
nteractive distance measurements between two locations in the scene view. B=
ecause the input locations are 3D points, you can measure between locations=
 on, above, or below the surface.</p><aside role=3D"region" aria-labelledby=
=3D":R1lmuan35:" style=3D"--note-accent:var(--color-brand)" class=3D"note b=
g-inherit"><header class=3D"me-1 items-center gap-1 float-left inline-flex =
align-middle"><calcite-icon aria-hidden=3D"true" scale=3D"s" calcite-hydrat=
ed=3D"" icon=3D"information-f"><template shadowmode=3D"open"><!----><svg ar=
ia-hidden=3D"true" fill=3D"currentColor" height=3D"100%" width=3D"100%" xml=
ns=3D"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class=3D" svg " viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16"><!-=
-?lit$585206983$--><!----><path d=3D""></path><!----></svg></template></cal=
cite-icon><span id=3D":R1lmuan35:" class=3D"m-0 font-bold text-color-2 text=
-inherit inline-block after:content-[':']">Note</span></header><div class=
=3D"contents" style=3D"--calcite-icon-color:currentColor"><p>Because the me=
asurements are planar, accuracy is good only for relatively short distances=
. Terrain is not considered in the measurement.</p></div></aside><p>A locat=
ion distance measurement returns the following distances:</p><ul>
<li>Direct distance=E2=80=94The straight-line distance between the start an=
d end locations</li>
<li>Horizontal distance=E2=80=94The planar distance between the start and e=
nd locations as projected onto the surface of the earth</li>
<li>Vertical distance=E2=80=94The difference in elevation (z-value) between=
 the start and end locations</li>
</ul><p>As you update the measurement end location (perhaps by dragging on =
the display), the analysis displays a graphic with lines for direct, horizo=
ntal, and vertical measurements.</p><span><span class=3D"gatsby-resp-image-=
wrapper block" style=3D"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: au=
to; margin-right: auto; max-width: 505px; display: inline-block; max-width:=
 505px; margin-left: unset; margin-right: unset;">
      <span class=3D"gatsby-resp-image-background-image" style=3D"padding-b=
ottom: 154.653%; position: relative; bottom: 0px; left: 0px; background-ima=
ge: url(&quot;data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAABQAAAAfCAIAAAB=
